---
source: crates/lighthouse-cli/tests/it/surface.rs
expression: "printed(dir.path(), &args)"
---
Measure a decision's check against its labels.

Reports precision, FPR, recall, FNR, selector precision and coverage, each with a Wilson 95% interval; the Brier score, ECE and AUROC of the probabilities models gave, with how many subjects they answered; the model calls and their cost, and a verdict against the decision's evaluation policy (`rules."<id>".evaluation`). With --check, a candidate decision file is measured beside it and their agreement is reported. Labelled positives come mostly from flagged findings, so recall is an upper bound; --audit writes unflagged selected subjects for a person to label.

Usage: lighthouse decision eval [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>
          The decision, such as `design/prefer-method`

Options:
      --config <FILE>
          The project file; the project root is then the current directory
          
          [env: LIGHTHOUSE_CONFIG=]

      --labels <FILE>
          A table of reviewed findings (`fingerprint repo location verdict`). Default: `labels/<id>.tsv` when it exists

      --color <COLOR>
          Color of help and messages. `auto` honours NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE
          
          [env: LIGHTHOUSE_COLOR=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --repo <NAME=DIR>
          A repository the labels are about, `name=dir`, where `name` is the `repo` column of the table. Repeatable; default: this project

      --check <FILE>
          A decision file whose check is evaluated beside the current one

      --model <REF>
          A model to grade as the first layer of the decision, beside the current check: `<provider>/<model>@sha256:<hex>`. One fitted on labels is graded on those by refits that leave each fold out

      --audit <N>
          Write this many selected subjects the check did not flag to a table for a person to label (`TP` or `FP`), to measure recall

      --format <FORMAT>
          `text` for people, `json` for programs (its schema: `lighthouse schema`)

          Possible values:
          - text: The human form
          - json: One JSON document on one line
          
          [default: text]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  lighthouse decision eval design/prefer-method
  lighthouse decision eval design/prefer-method --check candidate.yaml --format json
  lighthouse decision eval design/prefer-method --repo cel=../cel --audit 50
